    {
      "title": "India’s cockroach protesters defy Modi’s crackdown – The Latest",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2026/oct/14/indias-cockroach-protesters-defy-modis-crackdown-the-latest",
      "description": "<p>A fresh wave of protests by India’s youth-led Cockroach movement has been met with a brutal crackdown by Narendra Modi’s government and the closure of a historic protest site in Delhi. Two of the movement’s leaders have been imprisoned for five days for violating suppression measures. The protests, which began over frustration about education and unemployment, are now focused on voting rights. Modi’s government denies claims it is seeking to disenfranchise millions of voters. But its treatment of protesters has been criticised by the UN and Amnesty International and raised concerns that India is sliding further into autocracy. Lucy Hough speaks to South Asia correspondent Hannah Ellis-Petersen <strong>– </strong><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/@todayinfocuspodcast\"><strong>watch on YouTube</strong></a></p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2026/oct/14/indias-cockroach-protesters-defy-modis-crackdown-the-latest\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-14T17:40:05"
    },
    {
      "title": "Leaders of India’s Cockroach party jailed for violating ban on protest site",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/14/leaders-of-indias-cockroach-party-jailed-for-violating-ban-on-protest-site",
      "description": "<p>Ashutosh Ranka and Saurav Das will spend spend five days in prison for entering Delhi’s Jantar Mantar – a historic protest site that has been closed by authorities</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/14/india-protests-election-commission-gyanesh-kumar\">Why Indian voters are on the streets</a></p></li></ul><p>Two leaders of India’s youth-led Cockroach movement have been sent to jail for five days for violating a ban on protests at a site in central Delhi.</p><p>A court ordered that Ashutosh Ranka and Saurav Das, leaders of the Cockroach Janata party (CJP) movement, spend five days in Delhi’s Tihar prison for jumping over police barricades, holding up slogans and waving the Indian flag at Delhi’s Jantar Mantar.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/14/leaders-of-indias-cockroach-party-jailed-for-violating-ban-on-protest-site\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-14T05:29:23"
